    "description": "Consider the following JUnit4 test\n\n@\nTest\n\npublic\n\nvoid\n\ntestIfShyIsStripped\n(){\n\nString\n\nhtmlwithSHY\n= \n\"<html><body>quite&shy;a&shy;long&shy;word</body></html>\"\n;\n\nDocument\n\nparse\n= \nJsoup\n.\nparse\n(\nhtmlwithSHY\n);\n\nString\n\ntext\n= \nparse\n.\nbody\n().\ntext\n();\n\nassertEquals\n(\n\"quitealongword\"\n, \ntext\n);\n}\n\nThis test fails as text is parsed as \nquite-a-long-word\nrather then it's actual textual representation that would have been \nquitealongword\nin any browser.\n\nPerhaps this is working as intended, but it would be interesting to understand the reasoning behind it."
